1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:03,773
Player one,

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:05,443
you're about to enter a game you

3
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:06,956
didn't know you were playing.

4
00:00:07,216 --> 00:00:08,422
It's called credit.

5
00:00:08,422 --> 00:00:10,898
And your score decides whether lenders,

6
00:00:10,898 --> 00:00:12,930
landlords, and insurers say yes,

7
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:15,152
and what it costs you when they do.

8
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:17,588
Most people never learn the rules.

9
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:19,444
Today, you'll learn all five.

10
00:00:20,844 --> 00:00:22,783
Welcome back to Broke to Balance.

11
00:00:23,043 --> 00:00:23,855
Quick note:

12
00:00:23,855 --> 00:00:25,332
this is educational,

13
00:00:25,332 --> 00:00:27,548
not personal financial advice.

14
00:00:27,548 --> 00:00:29,321
I'll use the common U.S.

15
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:31,241
FICO scale as the example,

16
00:00:31,241 --> 00:00:34,639
and other scoring models and countries differ.

17
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:37,777
First, what is a credit score?

18
00:00:38,037 --> 00:00:40,637
It's a three-digit number that summarizes

19
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:42,603
how risky you look to a lender.

20
00:00:42,603 --> 00:00:44,189
On the most common scale,

21
00:00:44,189 --> 00:00:47,360
it runs from three hundred to eight hundred fifty.

22
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:50,693
Lenders use it to decide whether to approve you,

23
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:52,806
and what interest rate to charge.

24
00:00:52,806 --> 00:00:54,791
Landlords and some insurers may

25
00:00:54,791 --> 00:00:56,328
look at your credit too.

26
00:00:56,588 --> 00:00:59,462
A higher score can mean lower interest rates,

27
00:00:59,462 --> 00:01:00,676
and over the years,

28
00:01:00,676 --> 00:01:03,167
that can save you thousands of dollars.

29
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:06,331
Here are the FICO ranges.

30
00:01:06,591 --> 00:01:09,624
Three hundred to five seventy-nine is poor.

31
00:01:09,624 --> 00:01:12,303
Five eighty to six sixty-nine is fair.

32
00:01:12,303 --> 00:01:15,195
Six seventy to seven thirty-nine is good.

33
00:01:15,455 --> 00:01:18,461
Seven forty to seven ninety-nine is very good.

34
00:01:18,461 --> 00:01:21,075
And eight hundred and up is exceptional.

35
00:01:21,335 --> 00:01:23,085
If you're lower than you'd like,

36
00:01:23,085 --> 00:01:23,742
don't panic.

37
00:01:23,742 --> 00:01:24,398
Scores move,

38
00:01:24,398 --> 00:01:26,421
and you're about to learn the levers.

39
00:01:27,821 --> 00:01:29,119
Why does this matter?

40
00:01:29,119 --> 00:01:31,221
Let's compare two borrowers taking

41
00:01:31,221 --> 00:01:33,384
the same twenty thousand dollar car

42
00:01:33,384 --> 00:01:34,682
loan over five years.

43
00:01:34,942 --> 00:01:37,327
Sam has a score around seven sixty,

44
00:01:37,327 --> 00:01:39,235
and gets a six percent rate.

45
00:01:39,235 --> 00:01:41,552
Alex has a score around six forty,

46
00:01:41,552 --> 00:01:43,188
and gets twelve percent.

47
00:01:43,188 --> 00:01:45,164
Those rates are illustrative,

48
00:01:45,164 --> 00:01:46,799
but the pattern is real.

49
00:01:47,059 --> 00:01:48,931
Sam pays about three hundred

50
00:01:48,931 --> 00:01:50,869
eighty-seven dollars a month,

51
00:01:50,869 --> 00:01:54,010
and about thirty-two hundred in total interest.

52
00:01:54,010 --> 00:01:57,218
Alex pays about four hundred forty-five a month,

53
00:01:57,218 --> 00:02:00,026
and about sixty-seven hundred in interest.

54
00:02:00,286 --> 00:02:00,957
Same car.

55
00:02:00,957 --> 00:02:01,704
Same loan.

56
00:02:01,704 --> 00:02:04,317
A score gap that costs Alex roughly

57
00:02:04,317 --> 00:02:06,781
thirty-five hundred dollars more.

58
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:10,619
Here's the recipe behind a FICO score.

59
00:02:10,879 --> 00:02:13,659
Payment history is thirty-five percent.

60
00:02:13,659 --> 00:02:15,299
Amounts owed is thirty.

61
00:02:15,299 --> 00:02:17,866
Length of credit history is fifteen.

62
00:02:17,866 --> 00:02:19,149
New credit is ten.

63
00:02:19,149 --> 00:02:20,717
And credit mix is ten.

64
00:02:20,977 --> 00:02:23,015
Other models, like VantageScore,

65
00:02:23,015 --> 00:02:25,181
weigh things a little differently,

66
00:02:25,181 --> 00:02:27,155
but the big ideas are the same.

67
00:02:27,415 --> 00:02:28,386
Five factors.

68
00:02:28,386 --> 00:02:29,282
Five levels.

69
00:02:29,282 --> 00:02:30,103
Let's play.

70
00:02:31,503 --> 00:02:33,269
Level one, the biggest boss:

71
00:02:33,269 --> 00:02:34,279
payment history,

72
00:02:34,279 --> 00:02:36,424
thirty-five percent of your score.

73
00:02:36,684 --> 00:02:38,156
The question is simple:

74
00:02:38,156 --> 00:02:39,372
do you pay on time?

75
00:02:39,372 --> 00:02:42,572
Even one payment that's thirty days late can hurt,

76
00:02:42,572 --> 00:02:44,748
and late payments can stay on your

77
00:02:44,748 --> 00:02:46,604
report for up to seven years.

78
00:02:46,864 --> 00:02:47,887
The fix is easy.

79
00:02:47,887 --> 00:02:50,446
Set up autopay for at least the minimum,

80
00:02:50,446 --> 00:02:53,325
and add a calendar reminder for the due date.

81
00:03:00,325 --> 00:03:01,069
Level two:

82
00:03:01,069 --> 00:03:03,228
amounts owed, thirty percent.

83
00:03:03,228 --> 00:03:06,207
The big idea here is credit utilization.

84
00:03:06,467 --> 00:03:08,419
That's how much of your available

85
00:03:08,419 --> 00:03:09,602
credit you're using.

86
00:03:09,602 --> 00:03:11,909
If your limit is three thousand dollars

87
00:03:11,909 --> 00:03:13,507
and you carry nine hundred,

88
00:03:13,507 --> 00:03:14,808
that's thirty percent.

89
00:03:15,068 --> 00:03:18,242
A common guideline is to stay under thirty percent,

90
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:20,108
and lower is generally better.

91
00:03:20,368 --> 00:03:20,939
Two tips.

92
00:03:20,939 --> 00:03:23,858
Pay down balances before the statement closes.

93
00:03:23,858 --> 00:03:26,015
And if you ask for a higher limit,

94
00:03:26,015 --> 00:03:27,981
don't spend more because of it.

95
00:03:29,381 --> 00:03:30,130
Level three:

96
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:32,748
length of credit history, fifteen percent.

97
00:03:33,008 --> 00:03:35,202
Lenders like a long track record.

98
00:03:35,202 --> 00:03:37,928
The average age of your accounts matters,

99
00:03:37,928 --> 00:03:41,318
and so does how long your oldest one has been open.

100
00:03:41,578 --> 00:03:43,445
So keep old accounts open if

101
00:03:43,445 --> 00:03:45,044
they have no annual fee,

102
00:03:45,044 --> 00:03:47,510
and use them for a small purchase now

103
00:03:47,510 --> 00:03:49,443
and then to keep them active.

104
00:03:50,843 --> 00:03:51,581
Level four:

105
00:03:51,581 --> 00:03:53,189
new credit, ten percent.

106
00:03:53,449 --> 00:03:55,084
When you apply for credit,

107
00:03:55,084 --> 00:03:57,032
the lender does a hard inquiry,

108
00:03:57,032 --> 00:03:59,169
which can dip your score a little.

109
00:03:59,169 --> 00:04:02,187
Several applications in a short time can add up.

110
00:04:02,447 --> 00:04:05,116
Checking your own score is a soft inquiry,

111
00:04:05,116 --> 00:04:06,387
and it doesn't hurt.

112
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:08,294
So check as often as you like.

113
00:04:09,694 --> 00:04:10,453
Level five:

114
00:04:10,453 --> 00:04:12,110
credit mix, ten percent.

115
00:04:12,370 --> 00:04:14,558
Lenders like to see you can handle

116
00:04:14,558 --> 00:04:16,232
different kinds of credit:

117
00:04:16,232 --> 00:04:18,549
revolving credit, like credit cards,

118
00:04:18,549 --> 00:04:19,966
and installment loans,

119
00:04:19,966 --> 00:04:22,026
like a car loan or student loan.

120
00:04:22,286 --> 00:04:24,220
But please don't take out a loan

121
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:25,732
just to improve your mix.

122
00:04:25,732 --> 00:04:27,062
It's only ten percent,

123
00:04:27,062 --> 00:04:29,420
and debt you don't need isn't worth it.

124
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,663
So how do you raise your score?

125
00:04:38,663 --> 00:04:41,041
Think of it as a game plan, not a trick.

126
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:42,564
Start with autopay,

127
00:04:42,564 --> 00:04:44,426
so every payment is on time.

128
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:46,787
Next, bring your balances down,

129
00:04:46,787 --> 00:04:48,549
so your utilization drops.

130
00:04:48,809 --> 00:04:51,322
Avoid opening several accounts at once,

131
00:04:51,322 --> 00:04:53,384
and dispute any errors you find.

132
00:04:53,644 --> 00:04:54,744
Then be patient.

133
00:04:54,744 --> 00:04:57,151
Most changes take months, not days,

134
00:04:57,151 --> 00:04:58,870
and good habits compound.

135
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:01,431
One more power-up:

136
00:05:01,431 --> 00:05:03,430
protect your credit from fraud.

137
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:04,463
In the U.S.,

138
00:05:04,463 --> 00:05:06,460
you can freeze your credit with

139
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:07,813
each bureau for free,

140
00:05:07,813 --> 00:05:09,681
which stops new accounts from

141
00:05:09,681 --> 00:05:11,355
being opened in your name.

142
00:05:11,615 --> 00:05:13,646
You can also set up fraud alerts,

143
00:05:13,646 --> 00:05:16,660
and review your statements and reports regularly.

144
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:19,012
If you spot something you didn't do,

145
00:05:19,012 --> 00:05:20,233
report it right away.

146
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:23,588
Now, how do you see where you stand?

147
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:24,756
In the U.S.,

148
00:05:24,756 --> 00:05:28,235
you can get free credit reports from the three

149
00:05:28,235 --> 00:05:31,261
major bureaus at AnnualCreditReport.com.

150
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:32,892
Read them for mistakes,

151
00:05:32,892 --> 00:05:34,799
like accounts that aren't yours,

152
00:05:34,799 --> 00:05:36,408
and dispute anything wrong.

153
00:05:36,668 --> 00:05:39,026
Many banks and card issuers also show

154
00:05:39,026 --> 00:05:40,683
a free score in their app.

155
00:05:42,083 --> 00:05:43,748
What if you're starting from scratch?

156
00:05:44,008 --> 00:05:45,982
Three common ways to start:

157
00:05:45,982 --> 00:05:47,078
a secured card,

158
00:05:47,078 --> 00:05:49,198
where you put down a deposit.

159
00:05:49,198 --> 00:05:50,807
A credit-builder loan.

160
00:05:50,807 --> 00:05:53,365
Or becoming an authorized user on a

161
00:05:53,365 --> 00:05:54,974
trusted person's card.

162
00:05:55,234 --> 00:05:56,476
Whatever you choose,

163
00:05:56,476 --> 00:05:58,651
put one small recurring bill on it,

164
00:05:58,651 --> 00:06:01,074
and pay the full statement every month.

165
00:06:02,474 --> 00:06:04,189
Before we finish, four myths.

166
00:06:04,449 --> 00:06:05,037
Myth one:

167
00:06:05,037 --> 00:06:07,393
carrying a balance helps your score.

168
00:06:07,393 --> 00:06:08,112
It doesn't.

169
00:06:08,112 --> 00:06:09,879
It only costs you interest.

170
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:11,514
Pay in full when you can.

171
00:06:11,774 --> 00:06:12,307
Myth two:

172
00:06:12,307 --> 00:06:14,262
checking your own score hurts it.

173
00:06:14,262 --> 00:06:16,454
That's a soft inquiry, so it doesn't.

174
00:06:16,714 --> 00:06:17,478
Myth three:

175
00:06:17,478 --> 00:06:19,629
closing old cards always helps.

176
00:06:19,629 --> 00:06:21,780
It can shorten your history and

177
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:23,376
raise your utilization.

178
00:06:23,636 --> 00:06:24,569
And myth four:

179
00:06:24,569 --> 00:06:26,835
your income is part of your score.

180
00:06:26,835 --> 00:06:27,434
It isn't.

181
00:06:27,434 --> 00:06:30,233
Your credit report doesn't include income.

182
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:32,640
Let's recap.

183
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:34,773
Your score has five factors:

184
00:06:34,773 --> 00:06:36,780
payment history, amounts owed,

185
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:38,786
length of history, new credit,

186
00:06:38,786 --> 00:06:39,790
and credit mix.

187
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:42,177
Pay on time, keep balances low,

188
00:06:42,177 --> 00:06:43,206
and be patient.

189
00:06:43,206 --> 00:06:44,647
Time is on your side.

190
00:06:44,907 --> 00:06:46,362
If this helped, subscribe,

191
00:06:46,362 --> 00:06:47,928
and tell me in the comments:

192
00:06:47,928 --> 00:06:49,718
which level is toughest for you?

193
00:06:49,978 --> 00:06:52,726
And if you learned something from this video,

194
00:06:52,726 --> 00:06:54,864
don't forget to watch our next one:

195
00:06:54,864 --> 00:06:57,734
how to budget when you're paid every two weeks,

196
00:06:57,734 --> 00:06:59,138
or your income changes.

197
00:06:59,398 --> 00:07:01,944
This video is for education only and

198
00:07:01,944 --> 00:07:03,571
isn't financial advice.

199
00:07:03,571 --> 00:07:04,844
See you next time.

